# Words that must never appear in this repository, as HMAC-SHA256 digests of the
# lowercase word under a secret key, one per line. scripts/check-denylist.mjs
# splits every tracked text file, the fixtures and the built bundles into
# lowercase [a-z0-9]+ words and fails on any word whose digest is listed here.
#
# Why a key: a plain hash of a short word can be guessed back by hashing a
# dictionary, so a list of plain hashes publishes what it hides. An HMAC can not
# be attacked that way without the key.
#
# The key is not in this repository. A maintainer keeps it in the environment
# variable EPIC_PULSE_DENYLIST_KEY or in ~/.config/epic-pulse/denylist.key (mode
# 0600), and CI reads the repository secret DENYLIST_KEY. Without a key the check
# prints "denylist: skipped (no key)" and exits 0, so a contributor or a fork can
# still run the tests. CI on a push to this repository, or on a pull request from
# one of its own branches, has the key, and fails the check if it is missing
# rather than let the list go unchecked. `pnpm test` runs the check's own tests
# under a throwaway key; this list itself is checked only where the key is.
#
# To add a word, take its HMAC under the key in $KEY, of the lowercase word with
# no trailing newline, and put the digest (the last field of what openssl
# prints) on a line of its own:
#
#   printf %s word | openssl dgst -sha256 -hmac "$KEY"
#
# Never write the word itself here, in a commit message or in a pull request.
# Words are matched whole, so a name written in several words needs a digest for
# each word that must not appear, and one for the run-together form. A digest
# made under another key matches nothing, and the check still passes: when the
# key changes, every digest here changes with it.
